Tuning Internal Strain in MetalOrganic Frameworks via Vapor Phase Infiltration for $\mathtt{C O}_{2}$ Reduction
Strain is one of the most common physical phenomena in solid-state materials, and is fundamentally important for controlling the reactivity of catalysts. Progress in nanotechnology and advanced microscopic techniques have recently enabled the study of strain-tuned catalytic processes. However, while most previous studies have focused on modifying surface strain, this work concentrates on precisely controlling internal strain to advance the innate catalytic activity of a metalloporphyrin on MOFs. Vapor phase infiltration (VPI) method is utilized to produce metal oxide clusters inside the structure of MOF, which result in deformation of MOF and induction of internal strains. The strained catalyst demonstrated pronounced catalytic activity improvement compared to unstrained analogs, displaying the great potential of utilizing the VPI strategy to improve intrinsic catalytic performance of a variety of catalysts.
